# Calendar feeds: RFC 5545 requires CRLF line endings, and scripts/gen-ics.mjs emits CRLF.
# Without this rule, core.autocrlf=true normalizes them to LF when committing from Windows
# while the Linux CI runner (sync-ics.yml) stores CRLF — which churns all three feeds
# whole-file on every sync, and risks an LF-normalized feed reaching subscribers, where
# strict clients (Outlook in particular) can reject it. Keep the bytes exactly as generated.
*.ics -text

# Scripts must stay LF **in the working tree**, not just in the index. With core.autocrlf=true a
# Windows checkout rewrites them to CRLF, and a CRLF shebang line makes esbuild/vitest reject the
# whole file with a bare `SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token` and no line number — while plain
# `node` runs it fine. Verified 2026-08-24: an all-CRLF sync-lzv.mjs fails to import under vitest,
# and the same file with only the shebang line switched back to LF passes.
*.mjs text eol=lf
